description: one file per rule check, and the folder is the whole registry

A check decides whether one rule held on one edit. `rule-check.js` runs every
file here on the `PreToolUse` hook for Edit and Write, and `flow scorecard` adds
the results up.

Adding a check means adding a file. There is no list to register it in, for the
same reason skills have none: a second list is a second thing to update, and it
goes stale the first time somebody forgets.

Name the file after the rule id it enforces, so `no-git-mutations.js` holds the
check for `no-git-mutations`. The loader refuses a file whose `id` and filename
disagree.

**Read `references/write-checks.md` beside the `/file-findings` skill before
writing a line of code here.** It carries the one decision that comes first:
whether the rule is checkable at all. Finish this sentence in plain words before
anything else, and abandon the check if you cannot: *what is present in every
violation and absent from every clean example*. Most rules never get a check,
because no function can tell their violations apart. That is the normal outcome.

**Collect the examples off disk or out of git history, never from your head.**
An invented violation proves the check matches the pattern you already had in
mind. It says nothing about what an agent actually writes.

Each file exports:

    id       matches a rule id in a rule file, and groups the counts
    rule     path to the file holding that rule, relative to the Flow clone
    tier     measure, warn or block
    applies  (path, content) => is this rule relevant to this edit
    check    (path, content) => was it followed
    needs    'added' for only the text this edit introduces,
             'file' for the whole file as it will read afterwards
    message  the one line the agent reads on a violation
    since    the date the check last changed materially, so the scorecard
             throws away counts an older version produced

Every check starts at `measure`, which records and interrupts nothing, with
one exception two paragraphs down. Promotion to `warn` changes one word, and the
scorecard says when a rule has earned it. `block` waits until the false
positives are gone.

**A rule the user calls minor stays at `measure` whatever the scorecard says.**
Promotion buys an interruption, and an interruption is only worth it when the
rule matters more than the edit it stops.

**A path-scoped rule that matters starts at `warn` instead.** A rule carrying
`paths:` loads when Claude reads a matching file, and creating one is not a
read, so `measure` leaves a new file written with the rule absent. At `warn` the
hook injects the rule's whole text at the moment it is broken.
